 "literal": "Many things let them speak, those who do not know that. / The waterfall-brought fresh-budded eruvai grass, / the deep-pool-seeking elephant eats as mouthfuls, / of the lord of the mountain-rich land, the friendship / not reaching an end, well I knew, I indeed.",
 "translation": "Let those who do not understand speak as many things as they please. I know very well that my love for the lord of the mountainous land—where elephants seeking deep pools feed on mouthfuls of fresh eruvai grass brought down by the waterfalls—will never come to an end.",
 "modern": "விஷயம் தெரியாதவர்கள் பலவற்றையும் பேசட்டும். அருவி அடித்துக் கொண்டு வந்த புதிய மொட்டுகளையுடைய எருவைப்புல்லை, குளத்தைத் தேடிச் செல்லும் யானை கவளமாக உண்ணும் மலைநாட்டினுடைய தலைவனின் நட்பு, ஒருபோதும் முடிந்துபோகாது என்பதை நான் நன்றாகவே அறிந்துள்ளேன்.",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine, expressing her absolute confidence in the hero's love to her friend or to herself in response to the gossip of the villagers. She asserts that those who gossip do not understand the depth of their bond. The poem is set in the Kurinji landscape, which symbolizes union and deep intimacy. The image of the elephant seeking a deep pool and eating the fresh eruvai grass brought by the waterfall represents the hero seeking the heroine and enjoying her love, which is constantly nourished by natural, life-giving forces. The key words are 'கேண்மை' (friendship/love) and 'தலைபோகாமை' (not ending), emphasizing the eternal nature of their bond despite any external gossip.",
